<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Reformed Government of the Republic of China</h1><p class="st-meta">1938 CE – 1940 CE · capital Nanjing</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Japanese-sponsored puppet government for occupied central China, set up at Nanjing in 1938 in the aftermath of the Nanjing Massacre.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> in late 1937, the Japanese Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> Area Army sought Chinese collaborators to administer the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. On 28 March 1938 it inaugurated the Reformed Government of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China</a> at Nanjing, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liang-hongzhi">Liang Hongzhi</a>, a former official of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beiyang-government">Beiyang government</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The regime nominally governed occupied parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> and Anhui and the Chinese districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, but its ministers had little authority and its revenues depended on Japanese approval. Its members were mostly older politicians from the warlord era, and it had little public support in a region devastated by war.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In March 1940 it was dissolved into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wang-jingwei-regime">Wang Jingwei regime</a>, which took over its offices at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liang-hongzhi">Liang Hongzhi</a> held posts in the new government and was executed for treason in 1946.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/reformed-government-of-the-republic-of-china.jpg" alt="Reformed Government of the Republic of China" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Original: en:Provisional Government of the Republic of China (1912) Vector: Kibinsky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_China_(1912%E2%80%931928).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1938 CE – 1940 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nanjing</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Liang Hongzhi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese folk religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mandarin, Wu Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nanjing Reformed Government</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1939&amp;focus=reformed-government-of-the-republic-of-china" aria-label="Reformed Government of the Republic of China on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7736 1011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Reformed Government of the Republic of China lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8056 1267L8529 1145M8056 1267L8056 1267"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8529 1145h0M8056 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8056 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8056 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nanjing. <a href="/atlas?year=1939&amp;focus=reformed-government-of-the-republic-of-china">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
